    "summary": "An agent turns planners' plain-language input into constraints for a machine-scheduling optimizer that minimizes changeover costs.",
    "html": "<h2>Problem</h2>\n<p>Production planning has to react to things that change at short notice: machine outages, customer visits to inspect the product, shift changes. Planners describe these in plain language; the optimizer needs them as mathematical constraints. The goal is a production sequence across 5 parallel machines (~600 jobs over a two-week horizon, ~100k constraints) that minimizes changeover costs while honoring every constraint.</p>\n<h2>What I did</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Consolidated the data from several sources needed for planning</li>\n<li>Formulated a parallel machine scheduling problem in Google OR-Tools (CP-SAT)</li>\n<li>Built flexible constraint models that capture many different planning realities, including shift effects</li>\n<li>Built an agent (GPT-4o, ReAct, in Azure AI Foundry) whose tools are constraint types such as start-end windows, job ordering and machine outages: the agent picks and fills the right tool from the planner's request, and the tool writes the constraint into the optimizer deterministically</li>\n<li>Developed a multi-objective function balancing tardiness, earliness and changeover costs</li>\n<li>Shaped design and functionality of the planning app (React), where planners see the current plan and adjust it with their on-the-ground knowledge</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Evaluation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Compared baseline plans against optimized plans on selected time-stamped situations, using only the data available at that point in time</li>\n<li>Tested as a proof of concept in live operation</li>\n<li>Checked the agent's translations manually on a set of test situations</li>\n</ul>",
