    "html": "<h2>Problem</h2>\n<p>Each client had thousands of time series and a previous forecasting approach that was either manual or a simple model. No single model is best for every series and horizon, and planners need to know how uncertain a forecast is, not only its value.</p>\n<h2>What I did</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Implemented a variety of ML-based forecasting models in Python and R: random forests, boosting, Lazy Learning, Bayesian Regularized Regression, SVM, feed-forward neural networks. </li>\n<li>Implemented classical statistical models: smoothing (Holt-Winters, ETS), AR, ARIMA, GARCH</li>\n<li>Pre-processing pipeline optimized for monthly forecasts</li>\n<li>For the rail freight operator: workday adjustments to account for monthly effects caused by holidays and weekdays</li>\n<li>Rigorous evaluation approach for individual forecasts as well as the resulting ensemble, against the previous forecasts (manual or simple models, depending on the customer)</li>\n<li>Deployed on-premise or in Databricks, depending on the customer</li>\n<li>Used and refined across 5-6 engagements, among others a rail freight operator, the federal employment agency ((un)employment forecasts for a large number of cost centres) and a large bank</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Ensemble approach</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Rolling cross-validation to estimate each model's error, measured as root mean squared error (RMSE)</li>\n<li>Ensemble members receive their weight proportional to their performance on each time-series</li>\n<li>Additionally, weights calculated for different horizons to account for better short-term or long-term performance</li>\n<li>Variation in the weighted ensemble used to estimate the model uncertainty</li>\n</ul>",
