    "html": "<h2>Problem</h2>\n<p>Invoices and vendor documents arrive in every format and quality, from clean PDFs to phone photos with handwritten notes, and their fields had to be typed in by hand, around 100 hours a week. Some fields must be exactly right (amounts, parcel weights), others tolerate small errors (addresses), so plain accuracy was not a useful measure of quality.</p>\n<h2>Pipeline</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>2 months of a first LLM-based extraction with human oversight, to build a golden dataset with ground-truth fields</li>\n<li>Custom extraction score (ES), based on business logic and impact: crucial fields such as amount and parcel weight require an exact match, addresses are scored with the more forgiving Levenshtein distance. Combined into a weighted score between 0 and 1</li>\n<li>Trained on 4 weeks of documents, tested on the following 4 (time-based split)</li>\n<li>In production, spot checks and corrections from operations tracked the ES; retrained once it started dropping</li>\n<li>Scheduled extraction jobs, monitoring and retraining orchestrated with Airflow, running in Docker; prompt chaining and structured output with LangChain; every LLM call traced in LangSmith</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Approaches evaluated</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>OCR + frontier LLM with structured output (~80 % ES)</li>\n<li>Vision LLM + OCR + frontier LLM (~82 % ES)</li>\n<li>OCR + GPT-3.5 fine-tuned via the API, tuning epochs, learning rate etc. (~93 % ES)</li>\n<li>OCR + Mistral 7B fine-tuned with LoRA in PyTorch (~89 % ES)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>GPT-3.5 went to production: the easiest to deploy, with no major refactoring and no own hosting, and cheap to run.</p>",
